(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-pharm-e3-antiarrhythmic-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Antiarrhythmic Drugs — Clinical Vignettes 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 45-year-old woman with a seizure disorder develops ventricular tachycardia. Which drug's listed contraindications include her seizure disorder?","choices":["Lidocaine","Amiodarone","Procainamide","Sotalol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lidocaine's listed contraindications are seizures and age, and its toxicity runs from drowsiness to seizures. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with a structurally normal heart starts flecainide. Her next electrocardiogram shows longer PR, QRS and QT intervals. Which action explains these changes?","choices":["Sodium and potassium channel block","Beta-1 receptor block","Atrial potassium channel activation","Sodium-potassium ATPase inhibition"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Flecainide blocks sodium and potassium channels in the ventricles and slows sodium channel recovery, prolonging the PR, QRS and QT intervals. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man is receiving intravenous magnesium sulfate for torsades de pointes. Which listed toxicity is the most serious to watch for?","choices":["Pulmonary fibrosis","Agranulocytosis","Respiratory paralysis","Flushing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Magnesium sulfate's listed toxicities are bradycardia, flushing, headache and respiratory paralysis, the most serious of these. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman is starting long-term amiodarone. Which counseling point addresses a listed skin toxicity?","choices":["Expect flushing after each dose","Report a butterfly-type rash","Protect your skin from sunlight","Report ringing in your ears"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Amiodarone causes photosensitivity and a blue-gray skin color, so sun protection is the counseling point. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation also has heart failure with severely depressed pump function. Which drug is listed to be avoided in her?","choices":["Diltiazem","Digoxin","Amiodarone","Dofetilide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Diltiazem and verapamil are negative inotropes, to be avoided in depressed cardiac function. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman taking sotalol faints. The monitor shows a long QT interval and a polymorphic ventricular tachycardia twisting around the baseline. Which intravenous agent is listed for this rhythm?","choices":["Adenosine","Dofetilide","Verapamil","Magnesium sulfate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Torsades de pointes, which sotalol can cause, is treated with intravenous magnesium sulfate. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man takes digoxin and warfarin. He is started on amiodarone for recurrent ventricular tachycardia. What happens to his digoxin and warfarin levels?","choices":["Both fall","Digoxin rises, warfarin falls","Warfarin rises, digoxin falls","Both rise"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Amiodarone increases digoxin and warfarin, as well as flecainide, phenytoin and procainamide. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man has recurrent ventricular tachycardia that has not responded to other antiarrhythmic drugs. Which drug is listed for this situation?","choices":["Propranolol","Amiodarone","Verapamil","Adenosine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Amiodarone is listed for recurrent ventricular tachycardia or fibrillation resistant to other drugs. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with atrial flutter takes quinidine, metoprolol, warfarin and digoxin. He faints, and the monitor shows a long QT interval followed by a polymorphic ventricular rhythm twisting around the baseline. Which drug most likely caused this rhythm?","choices":["Quinidine","Metoprolol","Warfarin","Digoxin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Torsades de pointes (2 to 8%) and quinidine sudden death are on quinidine's toxicity list. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman known to be a slow acetylator has taken oral procainamide for several months. She now has joint pains, a rash and fever. Which adverse effect is most likely?","choices":["Cinchonism","Lupus-like syndrome","Agranulocytosis","Hypothyroidism"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A lupus-like syndrome affects 25 to 50% of patients on procainamide, more often slow acetylators. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man is receiving intravenous lidocaine for ventricular premature beats after an acute myocardial infarction. Which finding is the early sign of lidocaine toxicity?","choices":["Tinnitus","Corneal deposits","Flushing","Nystagmus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nystagmus is the early sign of lidocaine toxicity; drowsiness, confusion, restlessness, twitching and seizures follow. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man takes digoxin, and his ventricular rate in atrial fibrillation has slowed. Which action contributes to this effect?","choices":["Increased sympathetic tone","Increased parasympathetic tone","Beta-1 receptor block","Potassium channel block"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Digoxin inhibits the sodium-potassium ATPase, slows atrioventricular conduction and indirectly increases parasympathetic tone while decreasing sympathetic tone. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man has sustained ventricular tachycardia after a myocardial infarction. Which drug is listed to be avoided in his arrhythmia?","choices":["Amiodarone","Lidocaine","Verapamil","Procainamide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Verapamil and diltiazem are to be avoided in ventricular arrhythmias, sick sinus syndrome and depressed cardiac function. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with asthma has atrial fibrillation with a fast ventricular rate. A beta blocker is planned. Which beta blocker is preferred for him?","choices":["Propranolol","Sotalol","Propafenone","Metoprolol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Metoprolol is beta-1 selective and causes less bronchospasm than propranolol, which matters in asthma. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 29"}]};
